# db/migrations/.baseline — the pre-ledger past, declared once, WITH EVIDENCE.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ------------
# The list of migration files that were ALREADY APPLIED to the Itera database by
# hand, before itera.schema_migrations existed. On the FIRST run against a
# database with an empty ledger, apply-migrations.sh records exactly these
# filenames as applied WITHOUT EXECUTING THEM, and then applies everything else
# normally.
#
# WHY IT IS AN EXPLICIT FILE AND NOT A HEURISTIC
# ----------------------------------------------
# The obvious auto-reconciliation — "empty ledger means fresh install, so mark
# every file present as applied" — is exactly wrong, and would have been wrong on
# the day this was written: 0036, 0037 and 0038 were merged and NOT applied.
# Marking them applied would have made permanent and invisible the very gap this
# automation exists to close. The opposite heuristic — "empty ledger means apply
# everything" — is the pre-ledger behaviour we are leaving behind, and it would
# replay 36 files against production.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ 2026-08-12 — THE SECOND COLUMN IS NEW, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THIS FILE NOW
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Until today this file was a bare list of filenames, and "these were already
# applied" was a CLAIM WITH NO EVIDENCE ATTACHED. An empty ledger was taken as
# proof that the database was the pre-ledger production database. It is not
# proof. An empty ledger is also what a BRAND NEW, EMPTY database has.
#
# MEASURED, on a throwaway Postgres 17.6 on 2026-08-12, running
# `apply-migrations.sh apply` against an empty database:
#
#     [migrate] FIRST RUN — ledger is empty. Reconciling 36 baselined file(s)
#               as already applied, WITHOUT running them.
#     [migrate]   baselined 36 file(s) as applied at unknown (NOT executed)
#     ...
#     ledger_rows | itera_tables | itera_functions | billing_schema
#              36 |            2 |               0 |              0
#
# Thirty-six migrations recorded as applied. Two tables in the schema, and both
# of them are the ledger's own. Zero functions. No `billing` schema. The
# database that a disaster recovery would have produced was EMPTY, and the
# ledger asserted it was complete. (The run happened to stop at 0036 with
# `function itera.can_access_class_topic(text) does not exist` — created by
# 0022, which had just been marked applied without running. The error is luck,
# not a guard: it depends on a later file referencing an earlier one.)
#
# So each line now carries the OBJECT THAT PROVES ITS FILE RAN, and
# apply-migrations.sh checks every one before it baselines anything:
#
#   * every probe holds   -> this really is the pre-ledger database. Baseline.
#   * no probe holds      -> this is an EMPTY database. Do NOT baseline; apply
#                            every file normally, which is what builds a schema.
#   * some probes hold    -> REFUSE (exit 3). A half-built database is not a
#                            state this file can describe, and guessing between
#                            "apply" and "skip" is how one gets silently wrong.
#
# ONE-SHOT, still. This file is read ONLY when the ledger is empty. Once
# reconciliation has run it is inert, and NOTHING should ever be added to it — a
# new migration gets APPLIED, not baselined. It stays in the repo as the audit
# trail of what the reconciliation claimed, and now also of how that claim can
# be checked.
#
# FORMAT — `<filename>  <probe>`; `#` starts a comment; blank lines ignored.
#
#   table    <schema>.<table>
#   column   <schema>.<table>.<column>
#   function <schema>.<name>              (any signature)
#   policy   <schema>.<table> <policyname>
#   trigger  <schema>.<table> <triggername>
#   enum     <schema>.<type> <label>
#   none     <reason>                     -- the file creates NO catalogue
#                                            object, so its application cannot
#                                            be observed. Declared, not hidden.
#
# `none` is honest rather than convenient: those files are pure DML, so
# baselining them cannot hide a missing object, and re-running them is safe
# because every migration here is idempotent. They take no part in the vote.
#
# Verified against the live project (kjefmwlopzeaqgqtlbdn) on 2026-08-12, read
# only: every probe below holds, and NONE of the objects created by
# 0036/0037/0038 do.
#
# Both 0006 files are listed. That is not a typo: two different migrations
# claimed the same number, which is one of the reasons this ledger is keyed by
# filename rather than by number.

0001_itera_schema_v1.sql                  table    itera.tenants
0002_itera_rls.sql                        function itera.current_itera_user_id
0003_itera_seed.sql                       none     seeds tenant/program rows only; creates no object
0004_tenant_admin_metrics.sql             function itera.current_admin_tenant_ids
0005_tenant_admin_fixture.sql             none     verification fixture rows only; creates no object
0006_dfl_federation_dfl_batch.sql         column   itera.users.dfl_subject
0006_flatten_dfl_batch_spec_i18n.sql      none     rewrites activity spec JSON in place; creates no object
0007_dfl_batch_pilot_content.sql          none     seeds pilot content rows; creates no object
0008_xp_award_on_completion.sql           function itera.award_activity_xp
0009_dfl_federation_autoprovision.sql     function itera.handle_new_federated_user
0010_users_avatar_url.sql                 column   itera.users.avatar_url
0011_users_update_self_locale.sql         policy   itera.users users_update_self
0012_catalog_and_enroll.sql               function itera.catalog_programs
0013_class_sessions.sql                   table    itera.class_sessions
0014_activity_kinds_v1.sql                enum     itera.activity_kind video
0015_federation_plg_owner_leak_fix.sql    none     replaces the 0009 function body and re-creates its trigger; adds no new object
0016_activity_kind_sandbox_scenario.sql   enum     itera.activity_kind sandbox_scenario
0017_sandbox_evaluate_quality_gate.sql    none     updates activity spec JSON in place; creates no object
0018_class_sessions_podium_every.sql      column   itera.class_sessions.podium_every
0019_users_dfl_avatar_url.sql             column   itera.users.dfl_avatar_url
0020_class_answers_attendance.sql         table    itera.class_answers
0021_class_sessions_scheduled_at.sql      column   itera.class_sessions.scheduled_at
0022_realtime_class_channel_private.sql   function itera.can_access_class_topic
0023_realtime_class_control_topic.sql     function itera.can_control_class_topic
0024_activity_defs_reusable_library.sql   table    itera.activity_defs
0025_artifacts_learner_work.sql           column   itera.artifacts.tenant_id
0026_guest_link_live_session.sql          table    itera.class_guests
0027_artifact_policy_and_repo_path.sql    column   itera.activities.artifact_policy
0028_activity_results_truth_boundary.sql  none     a COMMENT ON TABLE only; the census covers it, a probe cannot
0029_class_stars_and_removals.sql         table    itera.class_stars
0030_backfill_activity_defs_titles.sql    none     backfills titles through a temp table; creates no lasting object
0031_activity_kind_concept_reveal.sql     enum     itera.activity_kind concept_reveal
0032_programs_archived_at.sql             column   itera.programs.archived_at
0033_activity_results_duration.sql        column   itera.activity_results.elapsed_ms
0034_activity_traces.sql                  table    itera.activity_traces
0035_activity_i18n_backup.sql             table    itera.activity_i18n_backup
